  "body": "Recently, when trying to enhance tokenizer tests, I've noticed that `</>` seems to be the only kind of content that is completely ignored at tokenizer stage instead of being treated as a text (like in `<>` case) or at least as a comment (like in some other invalid markup cases).\r\n\r\nGiven that we already fixed some inconsistencies in HTML spec, @inikulin suggested to open an issue to find out whether there is a legitimate reason behind that behaviour (not treating such combination as a text), or to see whether we could change the spec in a way that wouldn't break existing user content.\r\n\r\nAdmittedly, unlike parser errors, emitting text on this would be a user-facing and a potentially breaking change, but at least emitting a bogus comment shouldn't be a problem?",
